<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.1.3: Interpreting the Slope and Vertical Intercept of a Linear Model</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00695</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.1.3: Interpreting the Slope and Vertical Intercept of a Linear Model</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f968d3fa-66b0-4e56-a055-874be5d46e93</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The scatter plot shows the sale price of several food items, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and the cost of the
        ingredients used to produce those items, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, as well as a line that models the data. The
        line is also represented by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3.48</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.76</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <media alt="Scatter plot showing a positive correlation between ingredient cost and sales price in dollars, with a blue trend line indicating that higher ingredient costs correspond to higher sales prices.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_06_01.svg"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">What is the predicted sale price of an item that has ingredients that cost $1.50? Be
            prepared to show your reasoning.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00005">$5.98 from the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3.48</m:mn><m:mi>⋅</m:mi><m:mn>1.5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.76</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5.98</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, or approximately $6 from the
            scatter plot graph</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">What is the predicted ingredient cost of an item that has a sale price of $7? Be
            prepared to show your reasoning.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00008">$1.79 from the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="prefix">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>0.76</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3.48</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mn>1.79</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="true" fence="true" form="postfix">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mrow></m:math>, or
            approximately $1.75 from the scatter plot graph </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">What is the slope of the linear model?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00011">3.48.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">What does the slope value mean in this situation?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. This means that for
            every extra dollar spent on ingredients, the sale price of the food item is expected to
            increase by about $3.48</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00014">What is the 𝑦-intercept of the linear model?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00016">0.76</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00017">What does the value of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept mean in this situation?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. This means that a
            food item that is made with free ingredients would still be sold for approximately 76
            cents.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00019">Does the value of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept make sense in this situation?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>This doesn’t seem to make sense because something that is free to make should not
              cost anything for the customer.</item>
            <item>This might make sense since it would still cost some money to either run the
              machines or pay the people to make the item.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

      <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Using Linear Models</title>
      <para id="para-00021">Watch the following video to learn more about how to use linear models.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c7081f288d0cb5e03052a1f936334b0b53cd61ce"/>
      </media>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00003">
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
        <para id="para-00022"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_3_1_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
    </note>
  </section>

    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Interpreting a Linear Model</title>
        <para id="para-00025">The scatter plot below shows the height and speed of some of the world’s fastest
          roller coasters. A line has been drawn to show a good fit for the data.  </para>

          <media alt="A scatter plot that shows maximum height in feet on the x-axis and speed in miles per hour on the y-axis. The line drawn increases from left to right.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        <para id="para-00026">The equation of the line drawn is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>S</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.11</m:mn><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>60</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00027">What does this slope mean in the problem?</para>
        <para id="para-00028">For every foot taller the roller coaster is, the speed increases 0.11 miles per hour.</para>

        <note class="try look-at-me" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Interpreting a Linear Model</title>
        <para id="para-00029">Eight students were asked to estimate their score on a 10-point quiz. Their estimated
          and actual scores are given in the table below. Plot the points. Then sketch a line that
          fits the data.</para>

        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable unnumbered" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="2" align="center">
<thead>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> Predicted</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> Actual</emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
            </thead>
          
  <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry> 6 </entry>
                <entry> 6 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 7 </entry>
                <entry> 7 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 7 </entry>
                <entry> 8 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 8 </entry>
                <entry> 8 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 7 </entry>
                <entry> 9 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 9 </entry>
                <entry> 10 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 10 </entry>
                <entry> 10 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 10 </entry>
                <entry> 9 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>

        <para id="para-00030">Here are the points from the table plotted on a scatter plot, with the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis
          being the predicted scores and the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis being the actual scores.</para>

        <media alt="A scatter plot that shows predicted scores on the x-axis and actual scores on the y-axis. The drawn best-fit line increases from left to right.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_08_02.jpg"/>
        </media>

        <!--Text
        Entry Interaction Start -->
        <exercise id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00031">Tell what you would expect someone’s grade to actually be if they predicted their
              grade to be a 5 out of 10.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00032">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00034">Here is how to answer these questions using the linear model:</para>
            <para id="para-00035">Using the linear model above, if a student predicted a 5 on the quiz, they should
              have an actual score of 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <!--Text
        Entry Interaction Start -->
        <exercise id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00036">What does the slope mean in this problem?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00037">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
            <para id="para-00038">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00039">Here is how to answer these questions using the linear model:</para>
            <para id="para-00040">The slope is the change in the actual grade point for each predicted grade point.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
